@@@ hint
Lay the block out as `[rows pointers][data: rows*cols doubles]`. Total bytes = `rows * sizeof(double *)` (rounded up to `_Alignof(double)`) + `rows * cols * sizeof(double)`. After the `malloc`, set `m[i] = data + i * cols` for each row.
@@@ hint
Check every multiplication before you do it: `rows > SIZE_MAX / sizeof(double *)`, `cols > SIZE_MAX / rows`, and `rows*cols > (SIZE_MAX - table) / sizeof(double)`. Otherwise `1 x 2^61` wraps to a tiny allocation that "succeeds". Zero the data with a loop (or use `calloc`, but you still need the overflow checks to compute the size).
@@@ solution_code
#include <stddef.h>
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

double **matrix_alloc(size_t rows, size_t cols)
{
    if (rows == 0 || cols == 0)
        return NULL;

    /* 1. row-pointer table, padded so the data that follows is aligned for double */
    if (rows > SIZE_MAX / sizeof(double *))
        return NULL;
    size_t table = rows * sizeof(double *);
    size_t align = _Alignof(double);
    if (table > SIZE_MAX - (align - 1))
        return NULL;
    table = (table + align - 1) / align * align;

    /* 2. element data, with overflow checks on every multiplication/addition */
    if (cols > SIZE_MAX / rows)
        return NULL;
    size_t count = rows * cols;
    if (count > (SIZE_MAX - table) / sizeof(double))
        return NULL;
    size_t total = table + count * sizeof(double);

    unsigned char *block = malloc(total);           /* the ONLY allocation */
    if (block == NULL)
        return NULL;

    double **m = (double **)(void *)block;
    double *data = (double *)(void *)(block + table);
    for (size_t i = 0; i < rows; i++)
        m[i] = data + i * cols;
    for (size_t k = 0; k < count; k++)
        data[k] = 0.0;
    return m;                                       /* caller: free(m) once */
}

double **matrix_transpose(double **m, size_t rows, size_t cols)
{
    if (m == NULL)
        return NULL;
    double **t = matrix_alloc(cols, rows);
    if (t == NULL)
        return NULL;
    for (size_t i = 0; i < rows; i++)
        for (size_t j = 0; j < cols; j++)
            t[j][i] = m[i][j];
    return t;
}
@@@ explanation_md
**Approach.** Compute one size, make one allocation, then carve it up:

```
block: [ m[0] | m[1] | ... | m[rows-1] | pad | d00 d01 ... d0c | d10 ... ]
         \_____ rows * sizeof(double*) _/      ^ data, aligned for double
m[i] = data + i * cols
```

Because all rows live in one contiguous data area, `m[0]` can also be passed to code expecting a flat `double *` of `rows*cols` elements (BLAS-style), and one `free(m)` releases everything.

**Alignment.** On x86-64, `sizeof(double *) == 8 == _Alignof(double)`, so the padding is always 0. On a 32-bit MCU (4-byte pointers) with 8-byte-aligned `double`, an **odd** number of rows would leave the data misaligned. On Cortex-M4 that means a fault on `LDRD`/`VLDR`, or silently slow accesses. Rounding the table size up to `_Alignof(double)` keeps the layout portable.

**Overflow.** `1 x 2^61` doubles is `2^64` bytes, which wraps to **0** in 64-bit arithmetic. Without checks, `malloc(8)` succeeds and the "matrix" is a time bomb. Each `*` and `+` in the size computation is guarded by a division-based pre-check.

**Complexity.** Allocation O(rows * cols) (zeroing), transpose O(rows * cols). Space O(rows * cols) plus `rows` pointers, in one block.

**Common mistakes**
- Two allocations (the table plus a data block), freed with a single `free(m)`, which leaks the data.
- `malloc(rows * cols * sizeof(double))` without room for the pointer table, or vice versa.
- Unchecked `rows * cols * sizeof(double)`, which wraps on overflow.
- Ignoring alignment of the data part (harmless on x86-64 by coincidence, fatal on some MCUs).
- Assuming `malloc` returns zeroed memory.

**Interview follow-ups**
- Compare this layout with a plain `double *a` indexed as `a[i * cols + j]` (no table at all). When is the table worth its memory?
- Using a C99 pointer to VLA, `double (*m)[cols] = malloc(rows * sizeof *m);`, gives `m[i][j]` with one allocation and no table. Why do many embedded coding standards (MISRA C:2012 Rule 18.8) forbid VLA types?
- How would you implement the same single-block trick for an array of strings (`char **`) built from a list of words?
